WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.880
Breaking fourth walls for aliens, forgoing&nbsp;
science for love, and reality versus illusion!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.320
These bargain-basement endings left many&nbsp;
audiences feeling short-changed. But hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.081
at least we got to see Batman fake&nbsp;
his own death - so there's that!

00:00:14.081 --> 00:00:18.600
Directors have imagined the end of the world a&nbsp;
thousand times over, but audiences keep coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.040
back for more apocalyptic thrillers. A&nbsp;
doomsday film done right is always both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.540
thrilling and thought-provoking, encouraging&nbsp;
viewers to wonder what these visions of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:32.280
future reveal about the state of humanity today.&nbsp;
Of course, an apocalyptic movie that ends on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:36.480
sour note just feels like a shoddy attempt at&nbsp;
indulging in spectacle without offering anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.180
of substance. That's exactly where director&nbsp;
Alex Proyas' 2009 film "Knowing" falls flat.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.560
A young boy named Caleb discovers a page full&nbsp;
of mysterious numbers during a school event.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.380
His astrophysicist father, John, discovers that&nbsp;
they represent the dates of big disasters — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:57.300
that the biggest disaster of all, a massive&nbsp;
solar flare, will happen in mere days. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.560
before life on our planet is decimated, a&nbsp;
mysterious spaceship descends from the heavens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:06.480
with alien strangers beckoning Caleb and his new&nbsp;
friend, Abby, to come with them. The children are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.500
whisked away to an Earth-like planet without&nbsp;
their parents, and the film ends abruptly.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.940
As you'll see again later on this list, "it&nbsp;
was aliens" doesn't make for a particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.440
compelling twist ending. There's more&nbsp;
questions raised than answers given — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.433
it doesn't seem like anyone is clamoring&nbsp;
for answers after that disappointing finale.

00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:24.673
"What do you believe?"

00:01:28.113 --> 00:01:31.171
"I think… s--- just happens."

00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:36.660
The 1978 big-screen adaptation of "Superman"&nbsp;
starring Christopher Reeve is, without a shadow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:41.580
of a doubt, a stone cold classic and a major&nbsp;
landmark in the history of superhero media.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:46.140
The Richard Donner-directed film was one of the&nbsp;
first to approach its source material with both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:51.600
ambition and seriousness, resulting in a sincere&nbsp;
and reverent adaptation that opened the door for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.040
other filmmakers to do the same. Working&nbsp;
together, Donner and Reeve infused the Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.060
of Steel with humor and humanity, telling&nbsp;
the type of story that Superman fans had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.560
waited decades for. For all the many assets&nbsp;
and accolades held by "Superman," however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:10.380
it also comes with one massive liability to many&nbsp;
fans: its infamously cheap time-reversing ending.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.920
Like some of the other endings on this&nbsp;
list, "Superman" features an otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.180
complete finale to the film's events that's&nbsp;
quickly rewound and replaced with something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:23.220
less satisfying. Rather than see Superman deal&nbsp;
with the emotional turmoil of losing Lois Lane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:28.020
theatrical audiences were treated to the hero&nbsp;
literally altering the rotation of the Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.760
to do things over again. People could believe&nbsp;
a man could fly faster than a speeding bullet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:37.961
and punch more powerfully than a locomotive, but&nbsp;
reversing time was just a bridge too far for some.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:42.720
Despite an ending that feels disjointed&nbsp;
and incomplete, "10 Cloverfield Lane"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.100
is worth a watch for sure. The pacing in Dan&nbsp;
Trachtenberg's intense thriller never lets up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.120
and maintains an unrelenting, uncomfortably&nbsp;
tense atmosphere all the way through.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.800
After Michelle is knocked out in a car accident,&nbsp;
she wakes up in a strange bunker with two men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.180
she's never met. Howard, the owner of the bunker,&nbsp;
tells her that an event has occurred on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.500
surface that makes it uninhabitable, and that&nbsp;
she's only safe underground. As time goes on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.640
Michelle realizes that Howard is dangerous&nbsp;
— and if she wants a chance at surviving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.320
she'll have to take her chances topside.&nbsp;
She manages to barely escape Howard… but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.140
when she makes her escape and&nbsp;
finally emerges from the bunker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.100
it turns out that the doomsday prepper&nbsp;
may not have been so crazy after all.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.136
"'Crazy' is building your ark&nbsp;
after the flood has already come!"

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:30.600
That's right — it's aliens. After&nbsp;
barely avoiding a UFO's attacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.620
Michelle makes her way to Houston to aid the&nbsp;
survivors of the invasion, and credits roll.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.160
Audiences may not have been terribly&nbsp;
surprised by the sci-fi twist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.480
especially since the original "Cloverfield"&nbsp;
leaned into it hard. Still, a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.600
viewers held out hope for something&nbsp;
a bit more subversive and grounded.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:51.840
Steven Spielberg's 2005 film adaptation of "War&nbsp;
of the Worlds" follows Tom Cruise as Ray Ferrier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.340
a dockworker in New York who is estranged from&nbsp;
his daughter and son. He watches them while his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:01.020
ex-wife goes out of town to visit her parents&nbsp;
in Boston — and while she's gone, things go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:05.700
horribly wrong. After a series of lightning&nbsp;
strikes disrupt the power grid, some sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.860
of war machine rises from the ground and begins&nbsp;
vaporizing people, causing mass destruction. An&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:15.900
alien species is behind the attack, and Ray is on&nbsp;
a mission to protect his children and reunite them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:20.760
with their mother. In the end, the entire family&nbsp;
miraculously survives and finds each other in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.680
Boston, while the aliens are suddenly killed off&nbsp;
by the microbes that cover every surface on Earth.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:30.240
As it turns out, even Spielberg agrees with&nbsp;
fans who think the movie wraps up a bit too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:35.370
easily. The director is quoted in the book "James&nbsp;
Cameron's Story of Science Fiction" as saying —

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:39.000
"That film doesn't have a good ending. I never&nbsp;
could figure out how to end that darn thing."

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:44.280
Ever since "The Sixth Sense," it seems like most&nbsp;
of director M. Night Shyamalan's signature twist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.780
endings have been misses rather than hits.&nbsp;
Although "Signs" is generally regarded as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.100
standout entry in his filmography, accepting&nbsp;
the ending requires some serious suspension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:57.900
of disbelief. To be fair, it's a film about an&nbsp;
alien invasion, so it's not like every plot point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:03.180
could be 100% plausible — but there's a glaring&nbsp;
hole in the reason behind the aliens' demise.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.020
After extraterrestrials begin terrorizing&nbsp;
the planet and target his family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:11.640
former minister Graham Hess discovers that&nbsp;
simply splashing the aliens with water will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.480
cause them to react as though they've been&nbsp;
burned, eventually killing them. The aliens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:19.380
are now forced to quickly flee the planet&nbsp;
as they realize it's inhospitable for them.

00:05:19.933 --> 00:05:23.773
"There's a monster outside my&nbsp;
room, can I have a glass of water?"

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:30.420
Here's the problem: the Earth is 70% water,&nbsp;
and usually there's plenty of moisture in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:33.840
air. The aliens would have been exposed&nbsp;
to water the moment they exited their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.100
ships without suits. Does that make&nbsp;
"Signs" a bad movie? Not necessarily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:42.472
but its ending left a lot of people&nbsp;
feeling like the aliens: burned by H2O.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:48.180
The ending of David Fincher's 1997 thriller&nbsp;
"The Game" is an odd case. Since its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.920
fans and critics have voiced opposite opinions&nbsp;
about its effectiveness. One side considers it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:57.720
unsatisfying and invalidating, while the other&nbsp;
side maintains that the invalidation is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:01.980
whole point. Whether or not the ending is just&nbsp;
the intended final turn of the titular game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:04.860
it's almost impossible to deny&nbsp;
that it feels pretty cheap.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:09.540
The plot of "The Game" revolves around Michael&nbsp;
Douglas's character Nicholas becoming embroiled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:14.220
in a series of increasingly bizarre and&nbsp;
existentially confusing circumstances. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:19.080
ultimately include Nicholas in a live burial and&nbsp;
a rooftop fratricide — so when it's revealed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:23.340
everything was staged, no one was actually hurt,&nbsp;
and nothing happened as Nicholas thought it did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:28.320
a lot of viewers have been left feeling cheated.&nbsp;
Even Nicholas' final prize — a shirt that says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:32.640
"I was drugged and left for dead in Mexico&nbsp;
and all I got was this stupid T-shirt" — can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:36.360
come across like a pointed jab at everyone&nbsp;
who had become invested in the narrative.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.960
Some people adore the ending of "The&nbsp;
Game" for its boldness and absurdity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:43.832
but just as many were turned off by&nbsp;
its sudden and dramatic shift in tone.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:49.800
The "Indiana Jones" franchise has captivated&nbsp;
audiences for decades. It began in 1981 with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:54.240
"Raiders of the Lost Ark" and continued with&nbsp;
"Temple of Doom" and "Last Crusade" completing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.020
before the decade was over. Nearly 20&nbsp;
years passed without another sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:02.880
until "Indiana Jones and the Kingdom of the&nbsp;
Crystal Skull" premiered in 2008. An early scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:08.640
in which Indy survives an atomic explosion inside&nbsp;
a lead-lined fridge was the subject of hot debate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.560
but the ending was also so nonsensical that it&nbsp;
felt like a disservice to the entire franchise.

00:07:13.947 --> 00:07:19.167
"What exactly am I being accused of&nbsp;
besides surviving a nuclear blast?"

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:23.580
"Kingdom of the Crystal Skull" wraps up by&nbsp;
waving away the entire plot with a simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:29.040
yet overdone explanation: it was aliens. To top&nbsp;
it all off, after seeing a spaceship rise from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.000
underneath an ancient city and watching some&nbsp;
of his colleagues get sucked into a vortex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.500
the final moments essentially just show&nbsp;
Indy returning to normal life. Indy has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.800
witnessed some pretty wild supernatural&nbsp;
happenings in his first three adventures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.520
but somehow this sci-fi twist&nbsp;
feels like something else entirely.

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:49.200
The fan community's negative reaction to "Kingdom&nbsp;
of the Crystal Skull" is well documented at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.100
point. Whether it was the plot-armored&nbsp;
fridge or the extraterrestrial surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:57.540
it seemed like most people who saw the movie&nbsp;
when it came out had something to complain about.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.980
Just like the 2011 film "Limitless,"&nbsp;
the plot of the science fiction movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:06.900
"Lucy" relies on the premise that human&nbsp;
beings only use 10% of our brains. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:10.800
often-repeated "fact" is nothing more&nbsp;
than a myth — but even if you accept it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:15.420
as truth solely for the runtime of the film,&nbsp;
it still doesn't lead to a solid conclusion.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:20.400
The titular protagonist Lucy is kidnapped by&nbsp;
drug mules, and ends up ingesting an illicit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:25.260
substance that gives her the ability to use more&nbsp;
and more of her brain as time goes on. This gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:30.180
her superhuman powers, including telepathy,&nbsp;
telekinesis, and the inability to feel pain.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:34.980
In the final moments of the film, Lucy finally&nbsp;
brings her cognitive power to full capacity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:39.360
and she journeys back in time through the&nbsp;
cosmos to the moment of the Big Bang. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.980
physical body apparently becomes a flash&nbsp;
drive, yet she is able to send a text to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:47.520
police captain she had been working with&nbsp;
that simply states, "I am everywhere."

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:51.120
Talk about vague — it's unclear as&nbsp;
to why anyone thought that this film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:55.366
would be best wrapped up with an ambiguous,&nbsp;
bite-sized nugget of philosophy from Lucy.

00:08:55.380 --> 00:09:00.840
Have you ever seen a magician in action who was so&nbsp;
skilled at sleight of hand that for just a moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:05.520
you were convinced that magic was real? That's&nbsp;
basically the idea behind "Now You See Me," but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:10.560
kicked up more than a few notches. This infamous&nbsp;
film follows a group of four highly talented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:15.540
illusionists called the Horsemen, who perform a&nbsp;
signature, mind-blowing trick: they extract money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:19.740
from wealthy people's bank accounts and give it&nbsp;
to audience members. Naturally, the authorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:23.940
become suspicious of their activities,&nbsp;
and FBI agent Dylan Rhodes is on the case.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:25.500
By the end of the film, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:28.800
it's revealed that Rhodes himself&nbsp;
is actually an illusionist, too.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:35.580
"Oh my God… I did not see that&nbsp;
coming. That's impossible!"

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:40.080
Rhodes had been working behind the scenes&nbsp;
as the fifth Horseman the whole time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:44.580
avenging his father's death by stealing from&nbsp;
the people responsible. The Horsemen are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:49.080
invited to join a secret society called the&nbsp;
Eye, and the film turns from a heist thriller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:53.940
to something else entirely at the very last&nbsp;
second. With so little clarity over what's real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:58.609
and what's merely an illusion, it's no wonder&nbsp;
that audiences left theaters feeling confused.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:04.260
Based on the novel of the same name by Richard&nbsp;
Matheson, the post-apocalyptic film "I Am Legend"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:09.360
follows U.S. Army virologist Robert Neville as&nbsp;
he struggles to survive each day in an eerily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:13.800
empty New York City. The majority of those&nbsp;
who remain from a lethal viral outbreak have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:18.660
mutated into zombie-like creatures known as the&nbsp;
Darkseekers. Neville spends the majority of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:23.100
time trying to find a cure for the virus,&nbsp;
eventually capturing a female Darkseeker to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:27.000
experiment on. After three years with no one&nbsp;
but his German Shepherd to keep him company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:32.400
Neville finally meets two fellow survivors: a&nbsp;
woman named Anna and her son, Ethan. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:35.880
it's not long before the results&nbsp;
of his tests lead him to a cure.

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:40.140
When the Darkseekers come after Neville for&nbsp;
revenge, he's forced to sacrifice himself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:44.160
save Anna and Ethan, who protect the cure&nbsp;
and escape to a survivors' camp. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:47.580
the ending of the director's cut follows&nbsp;
the theme of the novel more closely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:51.540
and completely flips the perspective of&nbsp;
the film in the process. In that version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:56.160
Neville interacts with the alpha male of the&nbsp;
Darkseekers, and returns without finding a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:00.540
cure. He realizes that to the Darkseekers,&nbsp;
he has actually been the monster all along.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:03.300
Though the theatrical cut is&nbsp;
not without its defenders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:05.820
the only opinion more popular than preferring the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:09.840
director's cut is thinking that the ending&nbsp;
of Matheson's book wipes the floor with both.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:15.780
The 2003 extreme French horror film "High Tension"&nbsp;
really seems like it was made twist-first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:19.560
with a story built around it to allow&nbsp;
the reveal to happen. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:24.420
its ending has become one of the most commonly&nbsp;
cited in online discussions of bad endings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:28.320
even frequently proclaimed as one&nbsp;
of the worst twists of all time.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:33.300
To make a long and excruciatingly bloody story&nbsp;
short, the dramatic twist at the end of "High&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:37.440
Tension" is that the film's slasher killer&nbsp;
was secretly one of the movie's two apparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:41.160
protagonists. That probably would've been&nbsp;
fine, if it didn't mean that the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:46.080
events were physically impossible. Viewers are&nbsp;
expected to believe that a character is able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:50.640
to be in two places at once — and that didn't&nbsp;
sit well with many, regardless of how good the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:54.870
preceding hour might have been. Critic&nbsp;
Maitland McDonagh wrote for TV Guide —

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:57.600
"Unfortunately, they squander&nbsp;
their white-knuckle goodwill on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:02.220
an 11th-hour about-face so preposterous it&nbsp;
undermines everything that preceded it."

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:05.400
Perhaps with some more clever writing,&nbsp;
"High Tension" could have stuck the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:10.440
landing — but as it is, it's earned a pretty&nbsp;
understandable reputation as an illogical mess.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:13.500
In all fairness to the cast&nbsp;
and crew of "Star Wars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:17.340
The Rise of Skywalker," there is no single&nbsp;
ending that could have pleased every single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:21.540
one of the franchise's millions of fans.&nbsp;
Even with that in mind, however, it's hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:25.980
to call the ending the filmmakers ultimately&nbsp;
chose anything but cheap and unsatisfying.

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:29.340
"Somehow, Palpatine returned."

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:36.300
For a great many "Star Wars" enthusiasts,&nbsp;
the ending of "The Rise of Skywalker" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:40.680
the worst part of a trilogy that had already&nbsp;
featured several objectionable sequences. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:45.360
unlike the trilogy's other alleged faults, which&nbsp;
most often offended by ignoring characters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:50.940
canon from days of "Star Wars" past, the finale&nbsp;
committed an even worse, and even cheaper sin:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:56.160
it blatantly erased the overarching story&nbsp;
of its own trilogy. Rey being the child of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:00.420
"nobodies" showed that anyone can be a&nbsp;
Jedi, but "Episode IX" reversed that by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:04.260
making her the granddaughter of one of&nbsp;
the franchise's most powerful bad guys.

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:08.400
There are too many offenses to list in "The&nbsp;
Rise of Skywalker," from the awkward kiss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:13.020
to the fact that Palpatine is there at all —&nbsp;
but taken all together, it felt to many fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:17.220
that the ending was nothing more than a series&nbsp;
of rushed choices made to drum up nostalgia.

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:22.200
Alfred Hitchcock's "The Birds" — like the vast&nbsp;
majority of Hitchcock's films — is an excellent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:26.940
piece of cinema with an enduring legacy and&nbsp;
pervasive influence. It introduced mainstream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:31.560
audiences to an entirely new brand of horror,&nbsp;
one that generates effectiveness by remaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:36.480
vague throughout and never explaining itself.&nbsp;
A major component of that haunting ambiguity is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:41.160
the film's ending, which consists of a mere fade&nbsp;
to black after the main characters drive away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:45.720
Though the birds have ceased their assault for&nbsp;
the moment, there is no answer as to whether or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:49.800
not they'll begin again, only heightening the&nbsp;
terror. While the ending is an effective one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:54.360
it's also undoubtedly a cheap one — especially&nbsp;
given the fact that the original script included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:58.620
an answer and resolution involving a&nbsp;
car chase, which Hitchcock simply cut.

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:01.920
Film scholars and enthusiasts are&nbsp;
of two minds about the ending of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:05.040
"The Birds:" either its uncertainty&nbsp;
makes it thoughtful and effective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:09.660
or it finishes on an unsatisfactory note. No&nbsp;
one could ever dispute that it's a memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:14.340
and influential horror classic, but the quality&nbsp;
of its ending is certainly up for debate.

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:20.220
Based on Don Winslow's novel of the same name,&nbsp;
Oliver Stone's 2012 thriller "Savages" essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:24.660
devolves into little more than worn-out tropes&nbsp;
in the final scenes. Wealthy marijuana growers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:28.980
Chon and Ben, who are in a polyamorous&nbsp;
relationship with their girlfriend Ophelia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:34.080
get caught up with a violent Mexican drug cartel.&nbsp;
Ophelia is kidnapped and held against her will to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:37.680
prevent Chon and Ben from backing out of&nbsp;
the partnership and leaving the world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:42.180
crime. As the conflicts escalate, all of the&nbsp;
high-stakes drama culminates in a gunfight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:46.620
which naturally doesn't go as planned. Ben&nbsp;
is fatally wounded, and Chon administers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:50.760
a lethal injection to Ben, Ophelia, and&nbsp;
himself so that they can all die together.

00:14:50.981 --> 00:14:56.981
"That's the way I imagined it… but&nbsp;
the truth has a mind of its own."

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:01.800
Of course, the film ends up taking a different&nbsp;
turn. Ophelia wakes up very much alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:04.740
revealing that the previous scene&nbsp;
was nothing but a nightmare as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:08.880
DEA agents end up capturing some of the&nbsp;
high-ranking members of the cartel. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:12.360
three escape to an undisclosed tropical&nbsp;
island, where they live on the beach.

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:14.520
So it turns out that the stakes were pretty low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:18.480
for the protagonists all along. It's no&nbsp;
wonder that "Savages" never received the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:22.440
same acclaim as many of Oliver Stone's&nbsp;
other movies, like "Platoon" and "JFK."

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:28.320
It's no secret that the ending to James Cameron's&nbsp;
1997 historical epic "Titanic" has been met with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:33.060
an ocean of deep, dark, frigid criticism since&nbsp;
its release. What may surprise you, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:38.100
is that two entirely separate sequences in the&nbsp;
finale have generated mass derision, and both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:42.540
for their own unique approaches of cheapness. Of&nbsp;
course, the infamous door scene leading to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:47.040
death of Jack has inspired an endless amount&nbsp;
of scrutiny, but so has the emotional wrap-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:51.780
sequence in the modern day, which ends with Rose&nbsp;
dying and meeting Jack in Heaven. That final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:56.040
moment would be incredibly sweet if she didn't&nbsp;
also have a husband up there waiting for her too.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:16:00.600
As anyone who's participated in discussions&nbsp;
about "Titanic" knows, though, it's the door&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:04.380
scene that has truly captured people's&nbsp;
attention. The question of whether or not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:08.100
both lovebirds could have fit on the floating&nbsp;
door together has been raised for decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:13.200
even prompting the Mythbusters to test it out&nbsp;
for themselves. In 2022, though, Cameron himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:18.240
revealed that he had funded a scientific study&nbsp;
with a hypothermia expert. He told Variety —

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:21.180
"We have done a scientific study&nbsp;
to put this whole thing to rest and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:23.160
drive a stake through its heart once and for all."

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:27.420
During his arduous preparations for&nbsp;
"Interstellar," Christopher Nolan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:31.500
went to great lengths to ensure the movie's&nbsp;
scientific accuracy. The director worked with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:35.760
Nobel laureate in Physics Kip Thorne, whose&nbsp;
influence on the project extended from its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:40.080
initial conception all the way through writing&nbsp;
and digital effects. The end result is a film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:46.200
deeply rooted in the known behaviors of stars,&nbsp;
gravity, black holes, and relativity — until its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:49.620
surreal ending, a sequence that many&nbsp;
fans consider frustrating and cheap.

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:55.260
After sacrificing himself by falling into a black&nbsp;
hole, astronaut Joseph Cooper finds himself in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.260 --> 00:17:00.120
five-dimensional tesseract made by time-traveling&nbsp;
humans from the future. By communicating with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:04.020
daughter in the past, Cooper is able&nbsp;
to save humanity — and in the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:09.120
he learns that the most powerful force in the&nbsp;
universe is not gravity or entropy, but love.

00:17:09.424 --> 00:17:15.424
"Love is the one thing we're capable of perceiving&nbsp;
that transcends dimensions of time and space."

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:20.400
The heartstring tugging ending of "Interstellar"&nbsp;
certainly has its defenders, but many viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:24.780
felt annoyed that the sci-fi epic didn't&nbsp;
stay rooted in scientific accuracy. Is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:28.260
a beautiful moment, or a disappointing&nbsp;
cop-out? That's up to you to decide.

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:33.600
Guillermo del Toro's 2017 fantasy romance&nbsp;
"The Shape of Water" is an excellent movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:36.600
with a solid amount of awards and&nbsp;
critical ratings that speak for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:39.600
themselves. There is simply no&nbsp;
deriding the Best Picture winner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:42.840
as a whole — but that doesn't mean that&nbsp;
every one of its parts is untouchable.

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:47.640
Throughout the film, protagonist Elisa and her&nbsp;
amphibious lover are pursued by the evil Colonel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:51.900
Strickland, who intends to dissect the creature&nbsp;
in the name of scientific progress. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:56.880
after Elisa is tragically shot, it's the Amphibian&nbsp;
Man who slices Strickland's neck instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:01.440
killing him in a twist of karmic justice.&nbsp;
Many viewers expected to see Elisa die in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:05.400
her lover's arms — but the actual ending&nbsp;
ended up being a bit more controversial.

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:09.120
Elisa seemingly dies in the water,&nbsp;
only to be magically resurrected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:11.760
and granted gills by the Amphibian Man. For many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:15.660
the sudden and unprecedented use of&nbsp;
a drama-neutralizing superpower to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:20.100
undo previous story beats felt like a cop-out&nbsp;
along the lines of Superman's time reversal.

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:23.400
So what would have been a better&nbsp;
finale? In her essay for High on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:25.740
Films, writer Pallavi Dandamudi wrote —

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:28.680
"Instead of the appearance of Eliza’s&nbsp;
gills, the movie could just have ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:32.700
with both of them in the water, holding on&nbsp;
to each other. It would still be ambiguous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:36.240
but the implications of that ending would&nbsp;
be different and far more powerful."

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:41.880
One of the most disappointing, frustrating types&nbsp;
of cheap movie endings is when a film decides to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:46.020
end without addressing a main theme that it's&nbsp;
spent ample time setting up. It's worse when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:50.220
that theme was built up over multiple films&nbsp;
— a trilogy, for instance. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:54.120
it's even worse than that when the ending&nbsp;
directly contradicts the main theme instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:58.740
simply ignoring it. Unfortunately for many fans&nbsp;
of Christopher Nolan's "Dark Knight" trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:03.240
in their eyes, that worst-case scenario is exactly&nbsp;
what they got with "The Dark Knight Rises."

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:07.860
In order to prevent Bane's neutron bomb from&nbsp;
destroying Gotham, Bruce Wayne hooks it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:13.320
his airship and flies it over the bay. In his&nbsp;
final act as Batman, he sacrifices his life to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:17.280
save the people of his city. At least, that's&nbsp;
what the movie wants you to think at first.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:22.020
During the final scene in Florence, Italy,&nbsp;
Alfred sees Wayne alive and well. It turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:25.620
out that the hero actually faked his own&nbsp;
death, having fixed the autopilot function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:29.760
in his flying machine. Wayne's survival,&nbsp;
along with the tacked-on reveal that police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:34.500
officer John Blake's legal first name is&nbsp;
Robin, felt needless and pandering to many.

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:39.960
Jim Jarmusch is known for his thoughtful films&nbsp;
with independent sensibilities. His takes on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:44.100
genre fare, from the martial arts mob flick&nbsp;
"Ghost Dog: The Way of the Samurai" to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:48.780
vampire romance "Only Lovers Left Alive," have&nbsp;
been met with critical acclaim. That's why,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:52.680
when it was announced that Jarmusch's next&nbsp;
project would be a zombie comedy with what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:56.640
called "the biggest cast ever disassembled,"&nbsp;
fans of the filmmaker were pretty hyped.

00:19:56.640 --> 00:20:01.920
Starring everyone from Bill Murray and Adam Driver&nbsp;
to Danny Glover and Tilda Swinton, "The Dead Don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:07.020
Die" ended up being an entertaining, albeit slow&nbsp;
meta-comedy in which a shift in the Earth's axis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:11.400
has caused the deceased to start rising out&nbsp;
of their graves. So how did this rollicking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:16.560
undead romp turn so many people against it by&nbsp;
the end? No, it couldn't be. There's no way…

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:20.820
Well, if you can believe it, it's aliens&nbsp;
once again. If you're surprised to see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:24.780
UFO make an appearance in this zombie flick,&nbsp;
you're not alone — so are the main characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:28.560
who fully annihilate the fourth wall as&nbsp;
they remark on the bizarre plot development.

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:35.985
"Was that in the script?"
"No… not the one I read."

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:41.700
Jarmusch was almost certainly satirizing the trend&nbsp;
of twist ending aliens with his film's finale, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:45.900
many audience members who weren't in on the joke&nbsp;
left the theater with a bad taste in their mouth.

